deep.reconstruction:
Setting (from pp. 39-41). Grothendieck axiomatizes a structure (M, ≤, ≪, ≭, Λ ⊂ M) and asks when it 'comes from an atelier', the combinatorial model of a space built from 'lieux' that he develops in the 1986 Analysis situs notes. The data are:
- ≤ : an order on the lieux.
- ≪ : a weaker 'subordination' relation (axiom MΛ0: X ≤ Y ⇒ X ≪ Y).
- °≪ : a 'minimal' subordination. Y °≪ X' means X' is the least lieu below X to which Y is subordinate.
- Λ : a dense subset. Density means: for every X there is Z ∈ Λ with Z °≪ X.
- omb_Λ(X) = {Z ∈ Λ | Z ≪ X} : the 'shadow' of X on Λ.
- omb_Λ(X)° = {Z ∈ Λ | Z °≪ X} : its 'interior' part.

Page 40 first recalls, for the record, results already proved for ateliers on pp. 25-28, now restated in terms of shadows.

Lemma A (cf. Lemma 1 p.25). Assume X and Y have a common upper bound Z. Then the following are equivalent:
- X ≤ Y;
- X ≪ Y;
- omb_Λ(X) ⊂ omb_Λ(Y);
- omb_Λ(X)° ⊂ omb_Λ(Y);
- omb_Λ(X)° ∩ omb_Λ(Y) ≠ ∅.
So, inside a 'compatible' region, the order is detected by shadows on the dense set. In particular a single interior point of X's shadow lying in Y's shadow forces X ≤ Y. This is a strong rigidity, or 'separation', property.

Lemma B (cor. 1 p.28). If Y ≪ X, there is X' ≤ X with Y °≪ X'.

Lemma C (cor. 2 p.28). Let Y ≪ X and X' ≤ X. The following are equivalent:
- Y ≪ X';
- omb(Y) ⊂ omb(X');
- omb(Y)° ⊂ omb(X');
- omb(Y)° ∩ omb(X') ≠ ∅.

Corollary (cor. 3 p.28). With the same notation, the following are equivalent:
- Y °≪ X';
- omb(Y)° ⊂ omb(X')°;
- omb(Y)° ∩ omb(X')° ≠ ∅;
- X' is minimal in X̃^Y = {X' ∈ X̃ | Y ≪ X'};
- X' is the least element of X̃^Y.
Here X̃ is apparently the set of lieux ≤ X. The corollary says that the minimal subordinating lieux under X are unique and are detected by intersecting interior shadows.

Second half. Grothendieck introduces the relation ≭ and isolates which of its properties make the previous arguments (on the ligature relation ∥) work. He defines
 X ∥ Y ⇔ X ≭ Y and X̃ ∩ Ỹ = ∅.
That is, X and Y are 'compatible' but have no common sub-lieu: an adjacency or ligature relation.

Axiom MA0'. ≭ is reflexive, symmetric and hereditary downward: if X ≭ Y, X' ≤ X and Y' ≤ Y, then X' ≭ Y'. Comparing with p.41 (Lemma A' = Lemma A with only X ≭ Y assumed), ≭ is an abstract weakening of 'having a common upper bound'.

Axiom MA4 ('axiom of disjunction'). It is half-struck and garbled. Roughly: if X' ≪ X, Y' ≪ Y, and no X'' ≤ X' satisfies X'' ≭ Y (some auxiliary set being empty), then X' ∥ Y'. It is meant to produce ligatures from the absence of overlaps.

The purpose overall is a representation theorem. It would recover a geometric atelier, and its disposition of lieux and ligatures, from order-theoretic data plus a dense 'basis' Λ, in the spirit of reconstructing a space from a basis of opens. The next page adds MA5, saying that common shadows of compatible X and Y come from common sub-lieux, and uses Lemma A' to get uniqueness of minimal subordination.
